      "id": 424,
      "title": "Reconcile | FORGE Methodology | Embracing Digital",
      "summary": "Reconcile | FORGE Methodology | Embracing Digital Embracing Digital Home Guidance Briefings (EDTW) What changed Interviews (EDT) How others did it Architectural Deep Dives What to do next Learn Digital Transformation Overview Pillars AI, Cyber, Data, Edge, Computing, Comms ODXA Domains &amp; mappings Whitepapers Monthly synthesis Articles Explainers &amp; analysis Lectures (DTA) Learning series Community Community Newsletter Patreon LinkedIn About About Host Sponsors Shop Search Reconcile Align Intent Close the Gaps The Reconcile stage is the consensus and gap-resolution engine of the FORGE methodology within the GEAR framework. It compares empirical observations from the Find and Observe stages against strategic mission objectives to expose misalignments, resolve stakeholder conflicts, and negotiate architectural tradeoffs. By aligning executive intent with operational realities across the transformation dimensions, Reconcile establishes an agreed-upon, prioritized target architecture roadmap before significant capital is committed. Unify Disparate Teams Bridge the gaps between silos. Ensure that business leaders, architects, and operators are working toward the same mission-driven outcomes. Strategy vs. Reality Reconcile high-level executive strategy with the operational reality discovered in the previous stages. Adjust plans to account for real-world constraints. Shared Constraints Define the architectural \"guardrails\" that all teams must follow. These constraints ensure that separate initiatives still contribute to a cohesive whole. Key Activities Alignment Workshops: Facilitate sessions between stakeholders to reach consensus on priorities and trade-offs. Conflict Resolution: Identify where goals or technical choices overlap or conflict, and find a unified path forward. Roadmap Refinement: Update transformation plans based on what has been observed and reconciled. Governance Definition: Establish the decision-making frameworks that will guide the Ground and Enh",
