      "summary": "The FORGE Methodology: From Intent to Infrastructure (The GEAR Practice) | Digital Transformation Architect Embracing Digital Home Guidance Briefings (EDTW) What changed Interviews (EDT) How others did it Architectural Deep Dives What to do next Learn Digital Transformation Overview Pillars AI, Cyber, Data, Edge, Computing, Comms ODXA Domains &amp; mappings Whitepapers Monthly canonical synthesis Articles Explainers &amp; analysis Lectures (DTA) Learning series Community Community Newsletter Patreon LinkedIn About About Host Sponsors Shop Search العربية Deutsch English Français Italiano Português 日本語 The FORGE Methodology: From Intent to Infrastructure (The GEAR Practice) 2026-02-02 by Dr. Darren Pulsipher Download PDF Digital transformation often stalls because organizations lack a repeatable, practitioner-led method for moving from high-level models to operational reality. While the Open Digital Transformation Architecture (O-DXA) provides the structural model for the enterprise, the FORGE (Find, Observe, Reconcile, Ground, Enhance) methodology provides the active practice. Together, they form GEAR: The Transformation Operating System (TOS) —a comprehensive framework for bridging the gap between mission intent and infrastructure. GEAR integrates The Four Aspects : the Structural Domains (the horizontal map), the Applied Practice (the FORGE methodology), the Execution Pillars (specialized capability views), and the Transformation Dimensions (the operating terrain). This whitepaper details each stage of the FORGE methodology within the GEAR framework, establishing it as the definitive practice for the digital transformation architect. 1. Introduction: The Practice of Transformation The primary challenge in digital transformation is not a lack of technology, but a lack of execution discipline . Organizations often mistake the adoption of modern tools—cloud, AI, and agile methods—for transformation itself. As established in the foundational analysis of transformation ",
