      "title": "The Emergence of the Global Data Network | Embracing Digital Transformation",
      "summary": "The Emergence of the Global Data Network | Embracing Digital Transformation Embracing Digital Home Guidance Briefings (EDTW) What changed Interviews (EDT) How others did it Architectural Deep Dives What to do next Learn Digital Transformation Overview Pillars AI, Cyber, Data, Edge, Computing, Comms ODXA Domains &amp; mappings Whitepapers Monthly canonical synthesis Articles Explainers &amp; analysis Lectures (DTA) Learning series Community Community Newsletter Patreon LinkedIn About About Host Sponsors Shop Search Skip to content Home Episodes Episode 98: The Emergence of the Global Data Network Episode 98 The Emergence of the Global Data Network 2022-08-04T00:00:00.000Z Hosted by Dr. Darren Pulsipher Explore more in the episode archive . Summary In this episode, Darren reminisces with Chetan Venkatesh, CEO of MacroMeta. Venkatesh has a long history of data management from the beginning days of Grid Computing and has started MacroMeta to tackle data management across the globally dispersed edge, data centers, and clouds. moderncomputing edgecomputing dataprivacy multicloud zerotrust cybersecurity technology process Darren W Pulsipher Chetan Venkatesh Chetan is an engineer turned operations and start-up guy (Macrometa is his fourth startup). He says he has been working on the same problem of dealing with distributed data and reducing latency for twenty years. Data is no longer in a data center but everywhere: in the cloud, on the edge, and people’s laptops. Effectively managing all of that is a challenge. About ten years ago, Marc Andreessen said software is eating the world. At this point, the software has eaten everything and turned all kinds of constraints and barriers into opportunities. Multithreading computing is one of the barriers that has come down with the cloud. You can build applications that run in different parts of the world simultaneously. A developer movement is happening parallel to make everything as simple as it needs to be for the average compute",
